+### 3.11.1 Running the lanes and stages on separate instances
+
+`start.py` starts the API and the consumers four switches select
+(`src/utils/consumer_roles.py`). `REJECTION_ENABLED` (default `true`) and
+`DLT_ENABLED` (default `false`) choose the lanes; `FETCH_STAGE_ENABLED` and
+`ANALYSIS_STAGE_ENABLED` (both default `true`) choose which stages of those
+lanes run. A consumer starts when its lane and its stage are both on:
+
+| Consumer | Lane | Stage |
+|---|---|---|
+| `fast_consumer.py` (rejections -> `/fetch-logs`) | rejection | fetch |
+| `slow_consumer.py` (analysis queue -> `/analyze-rejection`) | rejection | analysis |
+| `dlt_consumer.py` (dead-letter topics -> `/fetch-dlt-logs`) | DLT | fetch |
+| `dlt_analysis_consumer.py` (DLT queue -> `/analyze-dlt`) | DLT | analysis |
+
+Left unset, the switches start what `start.py` always did. Set apart, they let
+one image run as separate Deployments, so what runs where, and the CPU,
+memory and replica count each kind of work gets, is chosen per Deployment:
+
+| Deployment | `REJECTION_ENABLED` | `DLT_ENABLED` | `FETCH_STAGE_ENABLED` | `ANALYSIS_STAGE_ENABLED` | Runs |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Log fetch, both lanes | `true` | `true` | `true` | `false` | API + `fast_consumer.py` + `dlt_consumer.py` |
+| Rejection analysis | `true` | `false` | `false` | `true` | API + `slow_consumer.py` |
+| DLT analysis | `false` | `true` | `false` | `true` | API + `dlt_analysis_consumer.py` |
+
+Every instance keeps its own API. A consumer only reads Kafka and forwards
+each message to the API over HTTP; the API is what fetches the logs, writes
+the casebook store and runs the agents. A consumer's entry script can be run
+on its own (section 4.1), but then nothing waits for an API to be ready
+before the consumer reads, and a message it cannot forward goes to the DLQ
+(`kafkaConsumer._process_and_commit`).
+
+`start.py` checks the switches before it starts anything, and on any error
+prints every error to stderr and exits 1:
+
+- **A split lane needs `CASEBOOK_STORAGE_BACKEND=s3`.** When an instance runs
+  one stage of a lane but not the other, the two stages hand every packet over
+  through the casebook store. The fetch stage saves `fetched_logs.txt`,
+  `status.json` and the packet's service resolution (in the DLT lane also the
+  headers, trace and deployed-version artifacts), and the analysis stage reads
+  them back. On local storage each instance has only its own disk, so the
+  analysis stage would find none of it and fetch the logs again itself, by
+  when the pods may have rotated them away. Nothing passes between the two
+  lanes, so an instance that runs both stages of each lane it runs needs no
+  shared storage for this. The `API_REPLICA_COUNT > 1` check in
+  `config_validator` does not catch a split: three single-replica Deployments
+  pass it.
+- **At least one consumer.** A combination that selects none is refused rather
+  than left running an idle API. The likeliest case is a DLT analysis instance
+  that turned the rejection lane off and forgot that `DLT_ENABLED` is off by
+  default.
+- **Each switch is `true` or `false`** (`1`/`0` and `yes`/`no` also work;
+  blank is the default). A misspelt value is refused rather than guessed: read
+  as off it would leave a lane unread, and read as on it would start consumers
+  that take a share of the partitions another instance was deployed to read.
+  This now applies to `DLT_ENABLED` too, which used to read any value other
+  than `true`, `1` or `yes` as off.
+
+Notes for separate Deployments:
+
+- Kafka divides a topic's partitions among the members of its consumer group,
+  so the replicas of one Deployment share its work, and replicas beyond a
+  topic's partition count sit idle. The three Deployments above share no
+  consumer group: each consumer runs on one of them only.
+- Neither lane's agents are built until the first analysis (`get_agent()`,
+  `get_dlt_agent()`), and only the analysis stage uses the agent tool server,
+  so the fetch Deployment can set `AGENT_MCP_SERVE=false` and skip that
+  process.
+- `MAX_CONCURRENT_INVESTIGATIONS` (and `MAX_CONCURRENT_DLT_ANALYSES`) is read
+  per process, so set it per Deployment.
+- More than one replica of the rejection analysis Deployment needs
+  `CHECKPOINT_BACKEND=postgres` (or `mysql`) as well as S3, as any API with
+  more than one replica does (section 1.3.4's scope limit).
+- `/health` still reports a heartbeat block for all four consumer roles; a
+  role that does not run on the instance reads `null` ("unknown"), as in any
+  split-pod deployment. Each consumer's own `*_HEALTH_PORT` answers for its
+  liveness.
+
